[la stela selckiku]
> Alright!  Hey, wait, you live in Egypt?  Um, how are things going over there?!
Apart from the government juggling their brains to satisfy the unsatisfiable 
masses, the jobs being terminated because the workers are 'demonstrating' most 
of the time and are expecting money to grow on trees, the mind-boggling number 
of 'free thinkers' who popped out of no where and are pretending they have a 
cause they can understand, we are pretty good! Don't alarmed by my sarcastic 
tone however, I'm merely letting it out. Me hopes it's the storm before the 
calm!
>...the biggest mistake made in the early history of Lojban was making too much 
>at once. The whole gismu list and cmavo for every imaginable possibility were 
>all invented and put in lists and then next everyone sat down to try to see if 
>they could say anything with them.
I imagine they wanted it to be ready, while not leaving it to the hands of time 
or users. It's a good point though, I'll it in mind.
> The main way I expect Lojban to change in the coming years is to develop much 
>more vocabulary. ...... People will always be saying, "You know what I think is 
>the  perfect word to describe that?" and then pulling out some rich obscure 
>precise descriptive term.
That may be, but I think that the problem with Lojban is that it wants to 
satisfy everyone. If they focused on a particular use for Lojban while in 
development, and built upon that as they gained experience, maybe into another 
bigger project, maybe it would have been better. You just can't have words that 
tells one meaning each, and expect to have words that equate to every thing 
expressible, at least that's what I think.
[clifford]
>This small vocal and growing may not be an ideal way to build a  language, but 
>it is to learn it.  Lojban seems to have followed Loglan  in making memorizing 
>the thousand plus gismu a precondition for full  participation.  I see that 
>folks are getting away from that, but I am  not sure there are useful guides 
>yet,
Sorry, what?
Have you seen my earlier post?
[Latro]
> There's a flip side to this in that essay by ..... other than  with regard to 
>things like jargon, or other highly specific things.
I'd agree on that too. Although I do like to use modifiers often!

PS. Has anyone seen 'mark'?

